    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the probability that the sum of the numbers shown on two standard 6-sided dice is a prime number, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the total number of possible outcomes:**\n   Each die has 6 faces, so when two dice are tossed, the total number of possible outcomes is:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 6 = 36\n   \\]\n\n2. **List the sums and identify which are prime numbers:**\n   We need to find all possible sums when two dice are rolled and determine which of these sums are prime numbers. The possible sums range from \\(2\\) to \\(12\\).\n\n   The prime numbers in this range are:\n   \\[\n   2, 3, 5, 7, 11\n   \\]\n\n3. **Count the number of ways to achieve each prime sum:**\n   - **Sum = 2:** (1, 1) \\(\\rightarrow\\) 1 way\n   - **Sum = 3:** (1, 2), (2, 1) \\(\\rightarrow\\) 2 ways\n   - **Sum = 5:** (1, 4), (2, 3), (3, 2), (4, 1) \\(\\rightarrow\\) 4 ways\n   - **Sum = 7:** (1, 6), (2, 5), (3, 4), (4, 3), (5, 2), (6, 1) \\(\\rightarrow\\) 6 ways\n   - **Sum = 11:** (5, 6), (6, 5) \\(\\rightarrow\\) 2 ways\n\n   Adding these, the total number of favorable outcomes is:\n   \\[\n   1 + 2 + 4 + 6 + 2 = 15\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the probability:**\n   The probability \\(P\\) that the sum of the numbers shown on the dice is a prime number is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes:\n   \\[\n   P = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{15}{36}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplify the fraction:**\n   Simplify \\(\\frac{15}{36}\\) by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 15 and 36, which is 3:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{15 \\div 3}{36 \\div 3} = \\frac{5}{12}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that the sum of the numbers shown on the dice is a prime number is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{5}{12}}\n\\]",
